zn-gate: disclosure for the npm dual-use content policy

Package:   zn-gate
Source:    https://github.com/usezn/zn
Security:  https://github.com/usezn/zn/blob/main/SECURITY.md

This package is a defensive security tool. Some of what it does can look like
malware to an automatic scanner, because protecting a computer from a coding
agent means touching the same places an attacker would: the configuration of
other programs, the permissions of files, and the way programs are started.
This file says what each of those things is for, and when it happens.

1. What zn-gate is

zn-gate is a local security layer for coding agents. It runs on the computer of
the person who installs it, between the agent and the files, commands and tools
the agent uses. It blocks the agent from reading files that hold secrets, hides
secret values in what tools return, checks text for prompt injection, reviews
MCP servers, and keeps secrets in an encrypted vault on the user's own computer.
It does not need an account. Its source code is open (MIT license).

2. Changes to the configuration of other programs

zn-gate adds hooks to the configuration of the coding agents it protects:
Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, OpenCode, Antigravity, the ZCode desktop app
and, as experimental support, Gemini CLI and GitHub Copilot CLI. A hook is an
entry that tells the agent to ask zn-gate before it runs a tool, reads a file or
sends a prompt. For OpenCode the hook is a small JavaScript plugin file that
zn-gate writes into OpenCode's plugins folder. The hooks start a short launcher
script that zn-gate keeps in its own folder (by default .zn/hooks in the home
folder).
The same setup can also, after asking:
  - register zn's "secrets" MCP server with an agent (for Claude Code, through
    the `claude mcp` command);
  - install, turn on or turn off the zn-gate plugin for Claude Code, through
    the `claude plugin` commands;
  - with `zn-gate init --wrap-mcp`, change the MCP server entries in the
    agents' configuration files (and in Claude Desktop's) so that each server
    starts through `npx -y zn-gate shield -- <the original command>`. zn-gate
    then checks what passes between the agent and that server; it starts only
    the server the person had configured.
This happens only when the person runs `zn hooks install` or `zn-gate init` in
their own terminal (`init` asks before each change). Nothing is written when
the package is installed or imported. zn-gate reads, but never writes, the
system-wide managed settings of Claude Code, Codex and Cursor, to report
whether they switch its hooks off; `zn team managed-config` only prints a file
for an administrator to put in place. `zn-gate uninstall` lists what zn-gate
added (hooks, wrapped MCP servers, the Claude Code plugin) and removes it after
asking. The hooks also guard their own files, so that the agent cannot switch
the protection off by editing them.

When an agent asks zn's secrets MCP server to write a file from a template,
zn-gate writes the secret values only for secrets the person set up for files,
and only into a file inside the project that git ignores (or that is outside
any repository). Overwriting a file, or writing outside the project, needs the
person's confirmation in a zn window or terminal, not the agent's.

3. Programs that zn-gate starts, and file permissions on Windows

zn-gate starts other programs by a fixed absolute path, or from the absolute
folders of the PATH, never from the project folder. It starts:
  - the agents' own command-line tools, for the setup steps in section 2;
  - npm, to find where zn-gate is installed (`npm prefix -g`) and, when the
    person accepts that step in `zn-gate init`, to install this same version
    for the user (`npm install -g zn-gate@<version>`);
  - git, to learn whether a file is inside a repository and ignored by it
    (`git rev-parse` and `git check-ignore`, with git's file monitor off);
  - Node.js, to run the vault as a background process after the person runs
    `zn unlock` (it stops when its time limit or idle limit passes), and to
    read the Node.js version in `zn-gate check`;
  - zn's own hooks, once each with a harmless test event, in `zn-gate check`
    and when the hooks for Antigravity are installed;
  - the command the person gives to `zn run`, with the stored secrets that
    command is allowed to use in its environment, and the MCP server the person
    configured, under `zn-gate shield`;
  - on macOS and Linux, the system's own tools: `security` (macOS) or
    `secret-tool` (Linux) to keep the vault key in the system keychain;
    `osascript` (macOS), `zenity` or `kdialog` (Linux) to show a confirmation
    window; `pbpaste` (macOS), `wl-paste`, `xclip` or `xsel` (Linux) to read the
    clipboard when the person passes --clipboard to store a secret; `open`
    (macOS) or `xdg-open` (Linux) to open the browser at the sign-in page in
    `zn-gate connect`; and `ps`, to read the list of running programs (process
    id, parent and command line) when a command that only a person should run,
    such as `zn-gate init`, `zn-gate uninstall`, `zn vault purge` or adding a
    secret, checks whether a coding agent started it. The list is kept in memory
    only; it is not saved or sent.

On Windows, zn-gate starts the PowerShell that ships with the system, by its
full path inside the Windows folder, never one found through the PATH or the
project folder. It runs short inline scripts with -Command or -EncodedCommand,
for these things only:
  - to make the vault and zn's hook files readable only by the current user.
    This uses .NET access-control calls that replace the permission list of
    those folders and files with a single entry for that user; zn-gate then
    reads the list back and checks it;
  - to give the vault's local named pipe the same single-user permission list;
  - to read, without changing them, the permission lists of a script and of
    every folder above it before the vault passes a secret to that script, and
    of the folder that holds zn's hook launcher and the folders above it, so
    that zn refuses a place that another account can change;
  - to keep the vault key in the Windows Credential Manager, through a few lines
    of C# that PowerShell compiles in memory to call the system's own credential
    functions;
  - to show a confirmation window;
  - to open the browser at the sign-in page when the person runs
    `zn-gate connect`, and to read the clipboard when the person passes
    --clipboard to store a secret;
  - to read the list of running programs (name, parent and command line), for
    the same check as `ps` above. The list is kept in memory only; it is not
    saved or sent;
  - to run zn's own hook once when the person runs `zn-gate check`.
The hooks that zn-gate writes for an agent on Windows also start this
PowerShell to run zn-gate. Nothing is downloaded for this, no user or group is
created or changed, and the permission changes apply only to zn-gate's own
folders, files and named pipe.

4. Deleting the vault

`zn vault purge` deletes the vault on the user's own computer (the encrypted
secrets, their token maps, the audit log, the backups and the session key) and
the vault key that zn-gate stored in the system keychain. It lists every file
it will delete, and the person has to type a confirmation phrase in their own
terminal. It refuses to run when an AI agent is the one running it (a script
with no terminal and no agent can pass --yes). It deletes only zn-gate's own
files and the keychain entry zn-gate created. `zn vault reset` deletes no
secrets: after the same kind of checks, it moves the old vault into a backup
folder with the date in its name, so that the next `zn unlock` starts an empty
vault.

5. No install scripts, no downloaded code, no telemetry by default

The package has no preinstall, install or postinstall script. zn-gate does not
download or run code from the network itself; everything it runs is in the
package or is a program already on the computer (section 3). The only downloads
it starts are steps the person accepts in `zn-gate init`: installing this same
version of zn-gate with npm, and adding the zn-gate plugin to Claude Code from
the usezn/zn repository with the `claude plugin` commands. npm and Claude Code
do those downloads. An MCP server wrapped by `zn-gate init --wrap-mcp` starts
with `npx -y zn-gate`, so npm fetches zn-gate from the npm registry when it is
not installed yet. The shield then starts each MCP server with the command the
person configured; when that command is a package runner such as npx or uvx,
the runner may fetch the server's code, as it would without zn-gate.

It collects no telemetry unless the person turns it on. Network use is limited
to features the person chooses:
  - the optional cloud check of prompts: after `zn-gate connect` it talks only
    to https://api.usezn.com; with an API key in ZN_API_KEY and ZN_CLOUD=1 it
    talks to https://api.usezn.com, or to the address the person sets in
    ZN_API_URL or --url. Secret values are removed from the text before it is
    sent. `zn-gate connect` itself talks only to https://api.usezn.com;
  - zn Team, after the person runs `zn team join`: zn-gate talks to
    https://api.usezn.com, or to the organization's own server given with
    --api (zn warns first), to fetch the organization's policy and to send the
    metadata of zn's audit records, never secret values or tool output;
  - HTTP requests that the secrets MCP server makes for an agent, only to the
    hosts the person allowed for each secret (`zn secret allow NAME --host`);
  - off by default, pseudonymous daily counts that `zn-gate stats share on`
    sends to https://api.usezn.com after printing them.
Hooks, the vault, the shield and every other command work locally. The vault
talks to zn-gate's own commands over a local socket in zn's folder (a named
pipe on Windows) that only the current user can open. The local dashboard
listens on 127.0.0.1 only.

